+# French-information follow-up: exhibits 59B, 346 and 642
+
+Research date: October 31, 2026, America/New_York. This continues the [published comparison of 336/337 against 326/636/637/639](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/385f959fd70d5caee75fe192325771ed7d8bdc9d/investigation/broader-evidence/moussaoui-french-controls-2026-10-30/README.md). That earlier reading remains unchanged: 336 contains a request, identification/photo material and a reference to an attached report's page 7; 337 contains an article representation. It did not claim that no substantive French reporting existed elsewhere.
+
+**The new court copies add substantive attributed reporting, a specific earlier-serial reference and an explicit draft warning.** Exhibit 346 reports French-source allegations on August 22. The August 30 EC in 59B reports later supplemental information, while the covering email in 642 calls its attached EC a draft and anticipates a later upload. These are concrete version, distribution and decision-record leads. They do not establish the historical truth of every allegation, who actually read each version, what a legal reviewer received or a deliberately suppressed opportunity.
+
+[Sources and acquisition identities](SOURCES.json), [physical-page and paragraph pins](SOURCE-PINS.json), [selected comparison and failed checks](COMPARISON-AND-FAILED-CHECKS.json), and [carried TODOs](OPEN-TODOS.json) preserve the distinctions. Source scans and all prior reports remain unchanged.
+
+## What exhibit 346 adds
+
+The [three-page August 22 EC](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/346.pdf#page=1) is an FBI representation of information attributed to French sources, rather than the request or article seen in 336/337. Page 1 has an August 22, 2001 date, a Paris origin, attention listings including Harry Samit and Mike Maltbie, a reference to Serial 1, and administrative references to August 16/17 calls and an August 22 fax. The case-number area bears a handwritten serial-looking 10. Printed approval/drafting fields and ink are preserved but are not independently authenticated signatures, upload records or recipient acknowledgments. Pins FR01–FR02.
+
+[Page 2](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/346.pdf#page=2) reports a multi-step association: a masked person reportedly worked for Khattab in Chechnya; a subsequent interview reportedly said someone had been recruited to go to Chechnya by Moussaoui and described him as dangerous. Identities and parts of the source chain remain redacted. This is substantive reporting to investigate, **not independent proof of the interview statements or an explicit, independently demonstrated bin Laden/al-Qaeda connection**. The readable passage does not itself establish a specific hijacking plan. No masked person is identified by combining this account with the similar biography in 337. Pin FR02.
+
+Pages 2–3 request passport travel-page photographs and a photograph of the second subject for further examination. These are requested inputs, not completed examination, delivery or receipt. The memo says no further information was provided at that time. The original fax, source interview and enclosure/distribution package remain missing. Pins FR01–FR03.
+
+**What this changes:** the earlier controls were insufficient to identify the underlying French intelligence; 346 now supplies an actual attributed substantive account. Its pre-August-24 face date and named attention fields make exact receipt and inclusion in the later review package important tests. **What it does not change:** naming a recipient does not prove reading, and a redacted reported association does not settle the legal-sufficiency dispute or authenticate every asserted relationship.
+
+## What 59B and 642 add
+
+The [seven-page 59B court copy](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/059B.pdf#page=1) has an EC dated August 30, 2001. Page 1 expressly references case 199M-MP-60130, Serial 10; it also describes information received August 29, an August 27 telephone contact, an August 22 fax at 1:10 PM and an August 30 Abbott–Maltbie telephone conversation. The enclosure field includes an original-serial-16 reference. These are more specific retrieval keys than subject/date resemblance, but neither the handwritten 10 in 346 nor the later serial reference independently authenticates a native package or proves delivery. Pin FR04.
+
+The translated block in 59B, pages 2–4, includes an interview dated September 28, 2000, descriptions of associations and radicalization, unfavorable source characterizations, and limits on recent direct knowledge. The later EC's August 29, 2001 receipt claim is distinct from the much earlier interview date. **Do not backdate availability of the supplemental information to August 22 or August 24 merely because it describes earlier events.** No original French interview/fax or qualified translation comparison was obtained. Redacted identities, kinship and agency/source affiliations are not reconstructed from differences in masking. Pins FR05–FR06.
+
+[642, physical page 1](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/642.pdf#page=1), shows Abbott's email to Maltbie and Samit, displayed Thursday, August 30, 2001, 11:32 AM. It describes the attached EC and French information. Its postscript expressly calls the attachment a **draft**, characterizes the translation as rough but sufficiently accurate, and says upload will occur **Friday or Tuesday**. This is an author's representation, not an authenticated native send timestamp, a certified translation, a completed upload or proof of recipient reading. The retained EC representations have formal drafting/approval fields; that appearance and the explicit draft warning must both remain in the record. Pin FR07.
+
+The substantive new version question is therefore **which attachment and translation became the uploaded/final record, when, and which version each recipient or reviewer received**. Neither court copy has been selected as controlling. Different display dates, formal fields, signatures, production labels and page layouts are not silently normalized into a finality or custody narrative. The native message/attachment, original French text, final upload and acknowledged recipient copies remain TODOs.
+
+## Information sharing and the proposed France option
+
+The source-protection paragraph in [59B page 4](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/059B.pdf#page=4) permits use while requiring that the information's origin be protected. The end of that page and [page 5](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/059B.pdf#page=5) additionally permit dissemination to British authorities as necessary under the same restriction. Corresponding passages appear in [642 pages 5–6](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/642.pdf#page=5); its cover email also reports British-sharing authority. Preserve the restriction **and** permission together. These passages do not support a blanket claim that all onward sharing was prohibited; they also do not prove anyone actually shared the complete packet. Pin FR08.
+
+59B page 4 and 642 page 6 report interest in exploring Moussaoui's deportation/transport to France and possible further custody/questioning. They describe a U.S. law-enforcement escort and transport of the laptop, mobile phone and other pertinent evidence to French authorities. Both say that evidence should **not be returned to Moussaoui** during the trip. Further arrangements are expressly conditional on the FBI pursuing that course. The leads in 59B page 6 and 642 page 7 ask Minneapolis, with headquarters concurrence/assistance, whether it wants to explore the option; other offices receive read-and-clear leads. Neither this wording nor the presence of leads proves a completed transfer, search, evidence handover, closed investigation or order to stop work. Pins FR09–FR10.
+
+This is a specific alternative-action lead worth tracing. Actual recommendations, responses, approvals/refusals, legal authority, transport/custody records and examination results would distinguish an explored option from an executed or rejected one. None was obtained here. The proposal weighs against a blanket assertion that nobody considered further investigative avenues, while leaving adequacy and follow-through unresolved.
+
+## Selected version comparison and the reading error we caught
+
+| Topic | 59B representation | 642 representation | Finding and limit |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Parent/context | Seven-page court EC copy; August 30 face date | Email plus EC representations; August 30 displayed email/EC dates | Same source family and repeated claims are not independent corroboration; native attachment identity/finality unverified |
+| Pagination | Header on physical page 1; translation on 2–4; option/permission on 4–5; leads on 6; visually blank 7 | Email on 1; EC header/start on 2; near-blank letterhead page 3; translation on 4–5; option/permission on 6; leads on 7 | Different layout/page roles, preserved without removing blank pages or treating them as proof of a removed substantive page |
+| Draft/approval context | Formal approval/drafting fields and visible ink | Same general formal fields, with covering email explicitly warning of draft status | No inferred final upload, approval authentication or preferred version |
+| Enclosure/marking | Page 1 exposes original-serial-16 reference; page 4 has printed M-MP1-80000080 and an additional 90000080 label | Corresponding enclosure area masked; page 6 has M-MP1-88000422 | Masking and numbering differences preserved; no reconstruction, typo theory, packet equivalence or alteration inference |
+| Evidence-return clause | Page 4 reads **not be returned to Moussaoui** | Page 6 reads **not be returned to Moussaoui** | Agreement on this selected clause; earlier suspected opposite wording was our reading error |
+| Sharing/transport | Source protection plus British-sharing permission; conditional France proposal | Corresponding permission, protection and conditional proposal; email also reports permission | Same broad readable propositions, not a complete character-by-character equivalence or execution finding |
+
+At the initial display resolution the agent suspected the 59B evidence-return sentence lacked a negation and noted the concern as pending. **Separate 3400-pixel whole-page renders, inspected at original resolution, visibly contain the negation in both copies. The suspected word-level discrepancy was not substantiated.** We preserve the failed check and its correction in [COMPARISON-AND-FAILED-CHECKS.json](COMPARISON-AND-FAILED-CHECKS.json). The source pages were never edited, reconciled or corrected. This is correction of our own reading, not adjudication of conflicting evidence. It must not be recycled as evidence of conflicting custody instructions.
+
+The selected comparison does **not** certify identical documents, translation accuracy or every character in faint/redacted passages. The independent 17-page visual reading and the selected clause reinspection remain different operations; rendering and hashing alone do not establish reading.
+
+## Comparison with 336/337 and the August 24 reports
+
+| Prior control | New comparison | What remains unresolved |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| [326, page 1](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/326.pdf#page=1): August 16 reply quoting August 15 inquiry and intended write-up/attempted handoff | 346 names earlier calls and an August 22 fax; 59B adds specific serial/date pointers | No explicit native manifest proves these are the exact promised write-up, complete handoff or recipient copy |
+| [336, pages 1/5](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/336.pdf#page=1): August 21 request and reference to an attached report's page 7 | 346 is a later substantive attributed report; 59B/642 contain a later supplement | These do not by themselves identify 336's missing report/page 7. The blank seventh physical page of 59B is not that referenced report page |
+| [337, pages 1–5](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/337.pdf#page=1): article representation | 346 contains a reported association and Chechnya biography, beyond the article's genre | Overlapping biography does not authenticate attachment identity, establish the article's assertions or identify a redacted person |
+| [636, page 1](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/636.pdf#page=1): August 24 report attributing a possible bin Laden link to French information and CIA/CTC assistance | 346 supplies a readable earlier attributed chain; later 59B/642 supply further descriptions | 346 is not automatically the complete assessment or CIA/CTC response. Lack of an explicit UBL assertion in its readable passages neither validates nor disproves 636's separate report |
+| [637, page 1](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/637.pdf#page=1): Paris-information attachment claim, reported headquarters position and disagreement | 346's named attention fields and later EC's Serial 10 reference are concrete package/recipient tests | Exact reviewed attachments, original advice and what each reviewer actually received/read remain unknown; later supplement cannot be retrojected |
+| [639, page 1](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/639.pdf#page=1): dissemination/tasking report alongside caution about planned wrongdoing | 346 reports an association/characterization; 59B/642 propose further investigation and permit sharing | An association allegation and an assessment of a concrete plan are different propositions. Conflicting assessments remain side by side; exact earlier communication referenced in 639, receipt and completed task returns remain missing |
+
+Pins FR11–FR13 identify the prior study's source pages and exact new comparison anchors. The six held controls are reused from their preserved acquisition/reading records; they were not redownloaded or claimed as fresh full readings in this unit. Prior bounded negative findings about 336/337 do not become false merely because another exhibit provides additional information.
+
+## Interpretation, confidence and next tests
+
+**High confidence, confined to the readable court representations:** 346 contains substantive source-attributed reporting; 59B identifies later supplement/serial/call/fax pointers; 642 explicitly calls its attachment a draft; permission/protection and conditional investigative proposals coexist; both selected custody clauses contain the negation. These observations do not authenticate native documents or establish the truth of the underlying allegations.
+
+**Limited inference:** the record supports a more specific inquiry into who received which intelligence and draft version before particular decisions. It does not yet show knowing omission, deliberate dilution, coordination or attack foreknowledge. To strengthen such an interpretation one would need a matched receipt/version/decision chain showing material received information knowingly omitted or misrepresented. A tentative or inaccurate source, a different actual review package, inclusion elsewhere, ordinary draft revisions or substantive follow-through could weaken it. These are untested alternatives, not explanations selected to resolve the record.
+
+**Priority TODOs:** native August 22 source fax/interview and enclosures; August 29 supplement/original-language text; 642's actual email/attachment and final uploaded EC with version log; exact recipient/legal-review copies and acknowledgments; responses/dispositions and actual custody/examination records for the France option; the complete investigative report/page 7 referenced in 336. [DRAFT-T01–T06](OPEN-TODOS.json) remain open with their earlier missing-input descriptions preserved. All eighteen original five-track criteria remain open or partial; this source review does not complete them or the separate FAA/telephone/recorder/materials/steel work.
+
+## Reading, preservation and verification scope
+
+Holdings were checked before acquisition: relevant active-study metadata and actual filename scans of the active research and verified Intel media trees found candidate/TODO links, but no confirmed target PDF/read-status record. An unrelated image whose name contained 59B was excluded. This was not a global search of renamed archives, private case files or every temporary copy; no global absence claim is made. Only the three actual court-index links were then acquired. Their total is **1,134,931 bytes, seventeen physical pages (7 + 3 + 7)**. The original sixteen-page default was prospectively extended by one page, before interpretation, to retain all three complete bounded entities.
+
+All seventeen physical pages were visually inspected at whole-page longest-dimension 1800-pixel rendering; two already-read pages were reinspected using 3400-pixel whole-page renders for the negation check. Blank/near-blank pages, different redactions, faint text and original page order are retained. No hidden-text extraction, OCR, masked-field recovery, face matching, original-language translation validation, native email/transport authentication, exhaustive pixel/text diff or historical-action test was performed.
+
+[SOURCE-CHECKS.json](SOURCE-CHECKS.json) records actual local byte/page/passive-container checks and render hashes. These establish preserved-copy integrity and the stated file/page inventory, not independent validation of manual readings, historical events or legal sufficiency. [CHECKLIST.md](CHECKLIST.md) accounts for the request. [TEXT-CHANGES.diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff) is a real Git addition diff against this new directory's immediately preceding absent state, not an invented native-email version diff. Prior source reports/TODOs remain byte-for-byte preserved; no earlier public text is replaced.
+
+A prior unit's failed whitespace precommit check and held-versus-new editorial corrections are retained with links in the failed-check record. The retained staged draft is untouched; no failure is silently turned into a passed publication. This unit's final frozen payload and actual commit/push/file verification are recorded separately.
+
+Public payload: appropriate original research notes, safe source URLs/hashes/page anchors, bounded comparisons, contrary findings, missing-input TODOs, method/failure records and text diff. Excluded: raw PDFs/rasters/full OCR, identifying/contact/passport/signature data, reconstructed redacted identities, lawsuit material, private correspondence/strategy and credentials. No outreach, new request, fee, filing, mailbox access, deletion, private push, force-push or merge.
